+plane.  Section 4 proves that **if** this missing statement holds, every \(g=2\) residual
+in question is impossible.
+
+## 4. Infinity-germ valuation test for the family-2 residual
+
+Here is the general valuation calculation.  Put \(u=3k\), with \(k\ge3\) odd.  The
+Abhyankar--Moh conditions force the minimal delta sequence to have the form
+\[
+\Delta=(6k,4,\eta),\qquad \eta\text{ odd},\quad 3k\le\eta<12k,quad
+2\eta\in\langle6k,4\rangle.                                                \tag{4.1}
+\]
+Indeed the gcd chain is \(6k,2,1\), with quotients \(3k,2\).  The conductor formula
+for this telescopic semigroup and the charged genus conversion give
+\[
+\delta_{\rm aff}={6k+\eta-3\over2},\qquad
+(a;\beta_1,\beta_2)=(6k-4;6k,18k-\eta).                    \tag{4.2}
+\]
+No choice of \(\eta\) is being suppressed.
+
+**Conditional NO-TORUS proposition.**  No residual curve with (4.1) and the charged
+affine double-point types can have an equation
+\[
+F=4A^3+27B^2,qquad \deg A=2k,quad\deg B=3k.               \tag{4.3}
+\]
+
+*Proof.*  Let \(Q\) be the unique point at infinity.  Irreducibility of \(F\) makes
+\(A,B\) coprime.  At an affine common zero \(P\), multiplicity two of \(F\) forces
+\(B=0\) to be smooth.  Put \(r_P=I_P(A,B)\).  In coordinates with \(B=y\), the first
+Newton form is \(y^2+\text{unit}\,x^{3r_P}\).  The residual requirement of two smooth
+branches forces \(r_P\) even and gives
+\(\delta_P=3r_P/2\).  Write \(\sum_{P\ne Q}r_P=2s\).  Other affine singularities can
+only increase \(\delta_{\rm aff}\), so
+\[
+3s\le\delta_{\rm aff}.                                                       \tag{4.4}
+\]
+
+Bezout and \(F|_{A=0}=27B^2\) give
+\[
+I_Q(A,B)=6k^2-2s,qquad I_Q(F,A)=12k^2-4s.                   \tag{4.5}
+\]
+Using (4.1), (4.2), and (4.4),
+\[
+I_Q(F,A)\ge12k^2-{4\over3}\delta_{\rm aff}
+=12k^2-4k-{2\eta\over3}+2>12k^2-12k+2.                    \tag{4.6}
+\]
+
+Choose local coordinates \((v,z)\) at \(Q\) with orders
+\((6k-4,6k)=2(\alpha,\beta)\), where
+\(\alpha=3k-2\), \(\beta=3k\).  Since \(2k<\alpha\), distinct monomials of total
+degree at most \(2k\) have distinct \((\alpha,\beta)\)-weights.  If the least local
+term of \(A\) is \(v^{q-j}z^j\), then
+\[
+I_Q(F,A)=2(q\alpha+2j),\qquad q\le2k.                       \tag{4.7}
+\]
+The left side is divisible by four by (4.5), while \(\alpha\) is odd, so \(q\) is
+even.  If \(q\le2k-2\), its value is at most
+\((2k-2)6k=12k^2-12k\), contradicting (4.6).  Hence \(q=2k\).
+
+The required first Newton face of the one branch is
+\((z^\alpha-\lambda v^\beta)^2\).  If \(j=0\) in (4.7), the first face of \(A^3\)
+has the same weight as that of \(B^2\); adding its nonzero \(v^{2\beta}\)-coefficient
+to a square makes the resulting quadratic in
+\(z^\alpha/v^\beta\) have two distinct roots.  If \(j>0\), \(A^3\) lies strictly
+above the first face, so
+\(B_{\rm face}=\mu(z^\alpha-\lambda v^\beta)\).  Since
+\(\gcd(\alpha,\beta)=1\), \(B=0\) has a primitive branch.  Along it
+\[
+I_Q(A,B)=6k^2-2s
+\]
+is even.  Newton--Puiseux applied after this approximate root therefore gives two
+corrections
+\(B=\pm c\,t^{3I_Q(A,B)/2}+\cdots\).  Its conjugacy group has odd order
+\(\alpha\), so it cannot exchange the two signs.  In both cases \(F\) has at least two
+places at \(Q\), a contradiction.  \(\square\)
+
+The affine hypothesis is essential.  Pure infinity orders do **not** prove this result:
+\[
+A_0=XZ^{2k-1},\quad B_0=X^2Z^{3k-2}-Y^{3k}
+\]
+give a degree-\(6k\) torus curve whose germ at \([1:0:0]\) is
+\(4z^{6k-3}+27(z^{3k-2}-y^{3k})^2\).  It is one branch with orders
+\((6k-4,6k)\), next characteristic numerator \(9k\), and numerically valid
+\(\Delta=(6k,4,9k)\).  But its affine equation has a multiplicity-three point at
+\((0,0)\), outside the residual two-branch class.  This witness is the negative control
+against promoting an orders-only argument.
+
